-- Passage rg7-literary-structure-pov-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('54757fb5-2297-5585-970f-f9c0d88d28be', 'rg7-literary-structure-pov-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Plot Fourteen$q$, $q$MATEO

Grandma Ines says the community garden opens at seven, so of course we are there at six forty-five. I wanted to sleep until noon. It is summer, after all. Instead I am kneeling in the dirt of plot fourteen, pulling weeds that look exactly like carrots. Grandma keeps humming an old song. I keep checking my phone, which has no new messages because everyone I know is still asleep. When she hands me a seed packet, I plant the beans without reading the directions. How hard can beans be?

GRANDMA INES

My grandson thinks I brought him here to work. I brought him because his mother says he has not looked up from a screen in weeks. When I was his age, my family grew everything we ate on a hillside smaller than this plot. I learned the names of plants the way he learns the names of games. Today he planted the beans too deep, and I did not tell him. In two weeks, when they do not come up, he will ask me why. Then, I think, he will finally be ready to listen.

MATEO

Two weeks later, the beans have not come up. Grandma's tomatoes are already as tall as my knee. I asked her what I did wrong, and she just pointed at the seed packet I never read. So now I am reading it. One inch deep, it says. I planted them almost a whole hand deep. Grandma is humming again, but this time I asked her what the song is about. It turns out it is about planting.$q$, 'draft')
