-- Passage rg7-literary-structure-pov-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5082df26-dd15-56b6-9671-375964d93845', 'rg7-literary-structure-pov-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Beaver and the Otter$q$, $q$Along a wide, slow river lived a Beaver who built and an Otter who played. Every morning the Beaver dragged branches to his dam and checked each stick twice. Every morning the Otter slid down the muddy bank, splashed, and floated on her back in the sun.

"You waste your days," the Beaver told her. "Rain is coming. When the river rises, you will have no shelter."

"And you waste your mornings," the Otter replied, "worrying about rain that has not fallen."

The Beaver shook his head and went back to work. The Otter rolled over and went back to floating.

Then the rains came, heavy and long. The river climbed over its banks, and the Otter's sunny bank vanished under brown water. She swam to the Beaver's lodge, cold and tired, and asked to come in. The Beaver let her rest by the dry inner wall.

But the water kept rising. On the third night it pushed against the dam until one corner began to give way. The Beaver could not patch it alone, because the current was too strong for him to swim against while carrying branches. The Otter had spent a thousand mornings playing in that very current. She dove beneath the surface and carried branch after branch into place. By sunrise, the dam held.

"I called your swimming a waste," the Beaver said quietly.

"And I called your building a worry," said the Otter. "It seems we were both half right."

Moral: Different strengths are worth the most when they are shared.$q$, 'draft')
