   $q$When Mom and Noor exchange looks at dinner, how does the narrator's reading of the moment differ from what the reader likely suspects?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The narrator thinks Noor is sick, but the reader suspects she is simply very tired.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The narrator and the reader both believe Mom is angry about the sawdust in Noor's hair.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The narrator thinks they are planning a surprise, but the reader suspects an argument.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-characters"},{"text":"The narrator thinks they are complaining about her, but the reader suspects a secret.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The narrator thinks they are complaining about her, but the reader suspects a secret.$q$, $q$The narrator decides "they were talking about how annoying I am," but the raised eyebrows and tiny head shake look like Mom checking whether the secret is safe. The narrator never suspects a surprise, so the reversed choice is wrong.$q$, 'draft', '9ec32c02-5e39-5842-9343-86d9641f2b9d')
